-## Workflow — spec first, scenarios drive the tests
-
-One-time: `npm i -g @fission-ai/openspec@1.12.0`, then `/opsx-setup` once per machine
-(team profile) and once per repo (init, `openspec/config.yaml`, `team-bdd` schema, scenario
-lint script; generated `/opsx:*` commands committed).
-
-| Stage | Command | What happens | Gate |
-|-------|---------|--------------|------|
-| Spec | `/spec <idea \| work item>` | `brainstorm` classifies (spike / bounded / architectural) → `grilling` frontier rounds → `domain-modeling` glossary → `/opsx:propose` under `team-bdd` → scenario lint + `openspec validate --strict` → design review by role | human approves the change; no code before |
-| Build | `/build <id>` | per task: tdd-guide writes the RED test from the scenario and its test-plan cases (`<Requirement>_<Scenario>`) → GREEN → Check → tick; slices one after another; deviations written back; bugfixes in fix mode | task ticked only when fully done |
-| Gate | `/gate <id>` | Check first (+ `openspec validate --strict` + scenario lint), then ONE code-reviewer pass (REVIEW.md passes incl. compliance vs the change, `security-review` preloaded) | Check GREEN, no open blocker |
-| Ship | `/ship <id>` | PR description from proposal + scenarios + evidence; the PR is created only after the user approves; post-merge `openspec archive` | the repo's CI review on PR open and push |
-
-Change hygiene, checked by `/spec` before it asks for approval: a change has one intent
-(more than three capabilities or twelve tasks means split it), requirements describe
-behaviour and leave paths and file names to `design.md` (the lint prints an `advisory:`
-line), a behaviour that can fail has a failure-path scenario, and one pull request carries
-one change. Changes are recorded under the schema in `openspec/config.yaml`, and the
-generated `.claude/commands/opsx/` and `.claude/skills/openspec-*` are committed; this repo
-follows both, and Verify-Config fails when either drifts.
-
-### Roles, one at a time
-
-Agents are chosen for their function and run one after another, each reading what the role
-before it produced. Parallel runs happen only when you ask for them or a stage command
-names the step; parallel writers still get their own worktree.
-
-| Stage | Role (agent) | What it contributes |
-|-------|--------------|---------------------|
-| `/spec` design review, architectural change | `architect` → `security-reviewer` → `test-planner` | structure, boundaries and trade-offs; then threats and abuse cases as candidate scenarios; then a test case per scenario with level, data and negative cases |
-| `/spec` design review, bounded change | `test-planner` (after `security-reviewer` when the change touches authentication, input handling, secrets or tenant data) | the test plan, saved as `test-plan.md` in the change |
-| `/spec` design review, spike | none | |
-| `/build` | `tdd-guide`, then the implementer | the failing test from the scenario and the plan's cases, then the smallest change that passes |
-| `/gate` | `code-reviewer`, which runs `security-reviewer` and then `database-reviewer` (dotnet plugin) when the diff calls for them | bugs, security, compliance with the change |
-
-Findings a role returns that tighten the design inside the approved intent are written into
-the change before you are asked to approve it; findings that change scope, cost or a
-decision you already made come to you first.
-
-`/feature <idea | id>` chains all four and resumes at the right stage. Repos without
-`openspec/` get the planner's five-part spec and an offer of `/opsx-setup`. Stage commands are
-user-invoked (hidden from the model's roster); the design skills (`grilling`, `grill-me`,
-`domain-modeling`, `brainstorm`) and the process skills (`tdd`, `verify`, `openspec`) are
-model-invoked so agents reach them too.
