-## Improvement loop — the environment learns, humans approve
-
-Team-core hooks record per-session **signals**: guard blocks (every guard script logs when
-it blocks), user corrections (a UserPromptSubmit detector for phrases like "no, that's
-not…", "revert", "don't"), and tool failures. Quiet sessions leave nothing. At SessionEnd,
-a session with signals launches a detached headless `claude -p /retro` that reads the
-signals and the transcript around them and writes **lesson candidates** — category
-(navigation · automated checks · reviewer standards · oversized steering · tool economy ·
-no-ops · information access · mistake twice), quoted evidence, target file, proposed
-change — to `~/.team-lessons/<repo>/` (manual runs: the repo's gitignored `.lessons/`; the
-Write tool refuses `.claude/` paths in headless mode). It never edits configuration.
-
-`/promote-lessons` shows pending candidates; the human accepts or dismisses each (with a
-reason that stops it being raised again). Project lessons land in the repo's CLAUDE.md
-"Things Claude gets wrong", `.claude/rules`, or `REVIEW.md` on a `chore/lessons-<date>`
-branch; team lessons become a branch in the ClaudeCodeConfig clone with the PR command
-printed. Shared skills change only through that path. `/retro` also runs by hand
-(`/retro --signals <file> --transcript <file>`).
-
