-## Hooks
-
-All hooks are node script files under `plugins/<name>/hooks/scripts/`, unit-tested by
-Verify-Config (never inline `node -e`: Claude Code runs hook commands through `bash -c`, and
-a raw backtick blocked every Bash call once).
-
-**A hook protects; it does not nag.** Every registered hook stops something dangerous and is
-silent otherwise. Quality signals — formatting, types, debug output, stray docs, task
-write-back — are not hooks: they belong to the project's Check command, to CI, and to the
-`/gate` reviewer. Hooks that interrupt the loop cost more attention than the defects they
-catch, so the team ships none.
-
-- **team-core**, and that is the whole list:
-  - shell commands that read secret files (`.env`, `appsettings.Production`, `.pfx`) — blocked
-  - foreground dev servers (`dotnet run|watch`, `npm run dev`, `vite`) — blocked, rerun in background
-  - hardcoded secrets in a Write/Edit payload — blocked
-  - dangerous git (`reset --hard`, `clean -f`, `checkout .`, `restore .`, `branch -D`,
-    `push --force`) — blocked, the user runs it themselves
-  - test files while `/build` fix mode is on — frozen, so a bugfix cannot edit its own
-    failing test (rare by construction)
-  - edits on `main`/`master` — blocked (branch first); a base not fetched in 24 hours is
-    fetched quietly before the edit, and an unreachable origin only leaves a note
-  - signals: guard blocks, user corrections, tool failures recorded per session; SessionEnd
-    launches the retro only when a session left signals (`RETRO_DISABLED=1` switches it off —
-    it is the only hook that spends tokens)
-- **dotnet**, **vue** — no hooks.
-
-Ordinary `git push` is not gated and PR creation is not blocked; the review that matters runs
-in CI on PR open and on every push, with `/gate` as the local pre-flight.
-
-A repo that wants more — an end-of-turn `Check`, a formatter, a type check — configures it
-in its own `.claude/settings.json` and owns the interruption. Team plugins stay silent.
